export const TEXT = "You're all set with Kilroy.\n\nKilroy tells you when the emails you send in Gmail get opened. Here's what to\nknow — the parts the extension can't fit next to a button.\n\nUSING IT\n1. Compose a message in Gmail. A \"Tracking\" chip appears next to Send.\n2. Send it. When it's opened, a double-check and a count appear on your sent\n   message, right in the thread.\n3. Open the dashboard from the Kilroy toolbar icon for opens per day, opens by\n   recipient, and every message you've tracked.\n\nWHAT AN \"OPEN\" REALLY MEANS\nAn open is an image being fetched -- not proof a person read your email. Kilroy\nsets aside the things that fetch images but aren't people: security scanners,\nApple Mail's privacy prefetch, and your own re-reads. It never invents a city or\na device. Treat the first-open time as the reliable number; counts are a floor,\nnot a total.\n\nYOUR DATA IS YOURS\nYour tracking is scoped to your account and isolated from every other user. Want\nto hold it entirely yourself? One click in the options points Kilroy at your own\ndatabase, and then nobody, not even us, can read it.\n\nGOOD TO KNOW\n- Turn tracking off for any single message from the chip beside Send.\n- Pin Kilroy: click the puzzle-piece icon in Chrome's toolbar, then the pin next\n  to Kilroy, so the dashboard is one click away.\n- Using your own database? Export your settings (Options > Preferences) so a\n  reinstall can't lose them.\n\nOpen Gmail and try it: https://mail.google.com/\n\n--\nMore at: https://kilroy.relaylabs.site\nOpen source: https://github.com/RelayLabs-apps/kilroy\nRelay Labs. You're receiving this because you set up Kilroy.\nTo stop these emails, reply with \"unsubscribe\".\n";
